    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Creativity is critical for all ages, and is essential for problem solving across a variety of disciplines. Like any other skill, it takes continual practice and exercise to maintain and develop. However, despite the fact that many often verbally proclaim its importance, it seems like it often takes a backseat to academic or procedural knowledge in practice. \r\n\r\nOne effective and important way to foster creativity is through imaginative play and storytelling. While education researchers may have different approaches to development, all agree on the importance of play. Thus, it is important to not only encourage play, but also to use open-ended play as an engaging way to keep developing creativity in children as they grow. While there are a few more open-ended tools designed to foster creative exploration in children in preschool and younger, fewer options are available for kids as they enter grade school. In fact, despite what we proclaim about the importance of creativity for development and problem solving, toy aisles for these ages still seem to be dominated by character-branded hot pink or dark blue toys. Such toys tend to have a pre-determined audience, purpose and story, and do not provide much educational or developmental value. \r\n\r\nKibuni is a building kit designed to engage children beyond kindergarten, and actually grows with the learner as he or she gets older. Of course, 4-5 year olds are welcome to play as well, though greater mentor guidance is recommended. The unique design lets users build a scene, structure, artistic expression, etc., the toy easily lends itself to storytelling. The extra layer of complexity opens up the play even further, engaging older children and adults alike. Finally, caretakers can take it one step beyond creativity to creative confidence by getting involved. To support this, the Kibuni | Teachable Moments website was created to support caretakers of younger childing by providing tips and ideas on how to engage in constructive creative play and storytelling. In additional, a social “Storyboard” connects projects created by the Kibuni community.",
